  "articleBody": "Framing your basement walls can feel like a big project, and it's easy to make a few common blunders that can cause issues later on. One big one is framing before you've tackled any water entry or humidity problems. Trust me, you don't want mold growing behind your brand-new walls! Another frequent slip-up is forgetting pressure-treated bottom plates or sill sealer when you're attaching to concrete. This is super important to prevent rot and keep you compliant with codes. We also see inaccurate layouts all the time, or folks forgetting to check for plumb and level. This leads to crooked walls, misaligned doors, and a real headache when it comes to drywall. Don't forget about utility access either! You need to think about clearance for your electrical panel, shutoff valves, and cleanouts. You don't want to cut into your fresh framing just to get to something essential. Finally, using the wrong fasteners into concrete, or not enough of them, can compromise the stability of your walls. Also, fire blocking is often missed, which is a big code violation and safety concern. When in doubt, or if the project feels overwhelming, it's always smart to get a professional opinion to make sure everything's done right the first time.",
